## From Gene Mutation to Disease

### Investigating How DNA Sequence Changes Affect Protein Products and Human Phenotypes

---

## Background

Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome is an inherited cancer predisposition condition commonly associated with pathogenic variants in the **BRCA1** and **BRCA2** genes. Individuals who carry pathogenic BRCA variants have an increased susceptibility to breast cancer and ovarian cancer and may also have increased risks in other cancers. Unlike sporadic cancers, hereditary cancer susceptibility can be passed from one generation to another because the predisposing genetic variant is present in the individual's inherited DNA.

BRCA1-associated cancer susceptibility is mainly related to the loss of normal mechanisms that maintain genomic stability. When the function is impaired, cells may become less capable of properly repairing certain forms of DNA damage. Accumulation of DNA damage and genomic abnormalities can contribute to the development of cancer. BRCA1-associated hereditary cancer susceptibility follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern.

