# Part II. Research the Disease

## A. Disease

Hereditary Breast and Ovarian Cancer (HBOC) syndrome is an inherited cancer-predisposition condition associated with pathogenic variants in genes such as **BRCA1**. In individuals with a pathogenic BRCA1 variant, the risk of developing certain cancers is increased, particularly breast cancer and ovarian cancer, including cancers of the fallopian tube and primary peritoneum. BRCA1-associated HBOC may also be associated with increased risks of other cancers, including pancreatic and prostate cancer, although the risks of other cancer types vary depending on whether the pathogenic variant is in BRCA1 or BRCA2.

The major clinical characteristic of HBOC is therefore a higher-than-usual lifetime susceptibility to certain cancers, rather than a single cancer that is always present. The condition follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern.

---

## B. Gene and Normal Protein

The **BRCA1 [BRCA1 DNA repair associated]** gene is a protein-coding gene located on chromosome 17q21.31. **NM_007294.4** is the reference transcript used that encodes the BRCA1 DNA repair-associated protein (**NP_009225.1**), a nuclear protein that functions as a tumor suppressor and helps maintain genomic stability.

BRCA1 participates in the cellular response to DNA damage, particularly the repair of DNA double-strand breaks through homologous recombination, and it also contributes to cell-cycle regulation, transcription, and interactions with other DNA-repair proteins. The BRCA1 protein is found predominantly in the nucleus, where it works with other proteins to detect and respond to DNA damage.

Through its involvement in DNA repair and genome-surveillance complexes, BRCA1 helps preserve the integrity of genetic information. Loss of normal BRCA1 function can therefore contribute to genomic instability and increased susceptibility to hereditary breast and ovarian cancers.

