# Part IX. Compare WT and Mutant Proteins

The WT and mutant BRCA1 protein sequences first differ at approximately amino-acid position 23, where the deletion causes a shift in the reading frame. More than one amino acid is affected because multiple downstream amino acids are changed after the mutation.

The mutation involves the deletion of two nucleotides rather than direct deletion or insertion of amino acids. The frameshift also produces a premature stop codon, causing translation to terminate earlier than in the WT protein and resulting in a shorter mutant protein.

Therefore, the mutation is classified as a frameshift mutation caused by a two-nucleotide deletion, which leads to extensive downstream changes in the protein sequence and premature termination.

