# Part X. Explain the Molecular Consequence

The documented BRCA1 mutation, **c.68_69delAG (185delAG)**, removes two nucleotides from the coding sequence. Because two is not divisible by three, the deletion changes the grouping of nucleotides into codons beginning at codon 23, producing a frameshift.

Alignment of the WT and mutant sequences shows that the amino-acid sequence begins to differ at approximately position 23, with multiple downstream amino acids altered and a premature termination signal appearing shortly afterward. ClinVar identifies this variant as **p.Glu23ValfsTer17** and classifies it as a frameshift or loss-of-function variant.

The frameshift changes the protein sequence rather than simply substituting one amino acid. Translation therefore produces an abnormal BRCA1 peptide that is much shorter than the normal, 1,863-amino-acid BRCA1 protein.

The normal BRCA1 protein is a nuclear tumor-suppressor protein involved in maintaining genomic stability, including DNA double-strand-break repair and homologous recombination. Because the mutation occurs very early in the coding sequence, the resulting sequence cannot produce the normal full-length BRCA1 protein through the original reading frame.

ClinVar indicates that the premature termination is expected to produce an absent or disrupted protein product, although some studies show that some 185delAG variants can produce an alternative RING-less BRCA1 protein through alternative translation.

Reduced or abnormal BRCA1 function can impair homology-directed repair of DNA damage. Normally, BRCA1 participates in the cellular response to DNA double-strand breaks and helps maintain genome stability. When this pathway is impaired, DNA damage can persist or be repaired through less accurate mechanisms, increasing genomic instability.

Published experimental work specifically involving BRCA1 185delAG found impaired homology-mediated DNA repair and increased genomic instability in human breast epithelial cells.

Over time, the accumulation of genetic abnormalities can increase the probability that cells acquire additional changes affecting growth and cell-cycle regulation, contributing to the development of BRCA1-associated hereditary breast and ovarian cancer susceptibility.

