# Part XIII. Interpretation Questions

## 32. Why does the exact location of a mutation matter?

The exact location determines which codon or functional region of the protein is affected. A mutation near the beginning of a coding sequence can alter a large portion of the protein, while a mutation in a less functionally important region may have a smaller effect.

---

## 33. Why can deleting three nucleotides produce a different result from deleting one or two nucleotides?

Three nucleotides correspond to one complete codon, so deleting exactly three nucleotides can remove one amino acid while preserving the downstream reading frame. In contrast, deleting one or two nucleotides shifts the reading frame and can change many downstream amino acids and introduce a premature stop codon.

---

## 34. Does every mutation change the amino-acid sequence? Explain.

No. A mutation can be **synonymous**, meaning that the altered codon still specifies the same amino acid because multiple codons can encode the same amino acid. Mutations outside coding regions can also occur without directly changing the protein sequence.

---

## 35. Does every amino-acid substitution destroy protein function? Explain.

No. The effect depends on the amino acid involved, its location, and the chemical properties of the substitution. Some substitutions have little effect on protein structure or activity, while others can interfere with an important functional or structural region.

---

## 36. Why can a frameshift affect many amino acids even if only one nucleotide was deleted?

Codons are read in groups of three nucleotides, so deleting one nucleotide changes how all subsequent nucleotides are grouped into codons. Consequently, many downstream codons can encode different amino acids until a stop codon is reached.

---

## 37. Why might a premature stop codon produce a nonfunctional protein?

A premature stop codon terminates translation before the normal protein is completed. The resulting truncated protein may lack essential structural or functional domains, and the mutant mRNA may also be recognized and degraded through nonsense-mediated decay.

---

## 38. Could a mutation affect protein function without greatly changing protein length?

Yes. A single-nucleotide substitution can change one important amino acid without changing protein length at all. If that amino acid is essential for catalytic activity, binding, folding, or interaction with another protein, the substitution can still substantially affect function.

---

## 39. Could a mutation cause disease without changing the protein sequence? Give a possible molecular mechanism.

Yes. A mutation in a promoter, enhancer, splice-regulatory region, or other noncoding region can change when, where, or how much of a normal protein is produced.

For example, a regulatory mutation could reduce BRCA1 expression and thereby decrease the amount of functional BRCA1 available for DNA repair even though the protein's amino-acid sequence remains unchanged.

---

## 40. What evidence from your analysis supports the proposed molecular mechanism of your disease?

The sequence comparison directly shows that the documented mutation removes two nucleotides, changes the reading frame beginning around codon 23, alters multiple downstream amino acids, and introduces a premature termination signal.

This computational result agrees with ClinVar's annotation of **c.68_69delAG** as a frameshift producing **p.Glu23ValfsTer17** and with experimental evidence showing impaired homology-mediated DNA repair and increased genomic instability associated with BRCA1 185delAG.

---

## 41. Which conclusions are supported directly by your computational results, and which require evidence from published experimental studies?

The computational analysis directly supports the DNA sequence change, reading-frame shift, altered amino-acid sequence, premature stop codon, and predicted change in protein length.

In contrast, conclusions about the actual effects on BRCA1 structure, DNA-repair activity, genomic stability, and cancer susceptibility require experimental and clinical evidence from published studies, because sequence analysis alone cannot demonstrate how the altered protein behaves inside cells.

